xis and ${y}_{i-1}$ and ${y}_{i}$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072435/assets/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x,y)=11x+7y+14$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=11x+7y+14$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072435/assets/67daa328cc6ae4cb37be386c327fb4548844fad2adc10476bdf1147c5a5d4218.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072435/assets/6e58954fccd74eaf67c95df818c4c9050c35b324c5fffe4902a366cb295b8845.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{ij} & =f({x}_{i},{y}_{j})=11{x}_{i}+7{y}_{j}+14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\le i\\le m$ and $1\\le j\\le n.$\n\nFinally, the upper Riemann sum is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(11{x}_{i}+7{y}_{j}+14)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $11{x}_{i}+7{y}_{j}+14.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377200 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "db8e7d68-5731-5a44-b86e-919a13689540" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Upper Riemann Sum for a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ec77b4a-2711-5859-914f-3c199a4c3d8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}_{i}^{2}+5{y}_{j}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377202} {:answer/id #uuid "1e379a78-d0c1-5af9-b0ba-a0435876ffc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11{x}_{i}^{2}+13{y}_{j}^{2}-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377203} {:answer/id #uuid "1aff8