}_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion ${R}_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$ over the $x$-axis and ${y}_{i-1}$ and ${y}_{i}$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072435/assets/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x,y)=135-4{x}^{2}-13{y}^{2}$ decreases as $x$ increases on $[1,2],$ and\n\n- $f(x,y)=135-4{x}^{2}-13{y}^{2}$ decreases as $y$ increases on $[2,3].$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072435/assets/ce0d90127e1f14654f4b32a933f1eb6e76e054c03f19ca40c3f04116aa3c2c93.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072435/assets/5b9967c814e46914e0409854de0960e85339adc9b9fd9693677c8e048c342702.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{ij} & =f({x}_{i-1},{y}_{j-1})=135-4{x}_{i-1}^{2}-13{y}_{j-1}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\le i\\le m$ and $1\\le j\\le n.$\n\nFinally, the upper Riemann sum is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(135-4{x}_{i-1}^{2}-13{y}_{j-1}^{2})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $135-4{x}_{i-1}^{2}-13{y}_{j-1}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5c07033-ef84-52e6-bd08-b1f0cc289e9f" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Upper Riemann Sum for a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/ch